menu "WiFi Dap core (CMSIS-DAP command engine)"

    config BIRDIEBOT_DAP_STATS
        bool "Enable DAP core profiling counters + Vendor9 stats command"
        default n
        help
            Debug-only instrumentation for OpenOCD/elaphureLink throughput work.

            Counts per-command processing time and the gap between consecutive
            host commands (transport round trip), the command-type histogram,
            0x7E/0x7F batch statistics, SWD transfer count and
            DAP_TransferBlock word counts.  A CMSIS-DAP Vendor9 command
            (0x89, sub-command 1 = snapshot, 0 = reset) returns the counters so
            a host-side raw-DAP client (elaphureLink port 3240) can read them
            remotely without serial console access.

            Production builds keep this disabled: no counters, no time reads,
            no Vendor9 handler.

    config BIRDIEBOT_SWD_ULTRA_FAST
        bool "ESP32-S3 ultra-fast dedicated-GPIO SWD engine (~20-24 MHz)"
        default y
        depends on IDF_TARGET_ESP32S3
        help
            Enable the M4 Xtensa dedicated-GPIO SWD backend
            (components/DAP/cmsis-dap/source/SWD_Fast_GPIO.c), which replaces
            the bit-banged SWCLK generation with fully unrolled single-
            instruction GPIO writes.  Target: ~20 MHz on the default 160 MHz
            CPU build and ~24 MHz when the CPU runs at 240 MHz, i.e. the same
            class as XRDAP-ESP's hardware-validated 23.7 MHz engine.

            This does NOT change the default SWD clock: the project default
            stays 2 MHz (docs/board-pins.md), and the engine is only selected
            when the host requests >= BIRDIEBOT_SWD_ULTRA_MIN_CLOCK_HZ via
            DAP_SWJ_Clock (OpenOCD `adapter speed`, target_flash or the local
            curve sampler).  Any failure (wrong core, pins not routed to the
            S3 dedicated-GPIO block, unsupported request) falls back to the
            existing GPIO fast backend.

            Default is y per the M4 user request ("enable the high-speed SWD
            engine by default"), but the engine is **not yet validated on the
            real board** (no logic-analyzer/HIL window in this phase).  Set
            this to n to restore the previous behaviour exactly; see
            docs/swd-23mhz-engine.md for the HIL checklist and the rollback
            procedure.  Only affects ESP32-S3 builds (JTAG, ESP32, C3 and C5
            are unchanged).

    config BIRDIEBOT_SWD_ULTRA_MIN_CLOCK_HZ
        int "Minimum DAP_SWJ_Clock request (Hz) that selects the ultra engine"
        default 12000000
        depends on BIRDIEBOT_SWD_ULTRA_FAST
        range 1000000 50000000
        help
            Lower bound for using the ultra-fast backend.  Requests below this
            value keep the existing backend selection (GPIO normal / GPIO
            fast / SPI >= 10 MHz), so low-speed debugging and the 2 MHz
            default are completely unaffected.

            12 MHz is slightly above the ~12-13 MHz effective SWCLK of the
            legacy GPIO fast path on the 160 MHz CPU build, so the ultra
            engine is only used where it can actually be faster.  The engine
            never exceeds the requested clock: a request of e.g. 15 MHz picks
            the 13.3 MHz level instead of the 20 MHz one.

    config BIRDIEBOT_SWD_ULTRA_OEN_STAY_ON
        bool "Keep SWDIO output enabled during SWD turnaround (A-fork workaround)"
        default n
        depends on BIRDIEBOT_SWD_ULTRA_FAST
        help
            SWD requires the host to release SWDIO during the turnaround and
            the ACK/data phases driven by the target.  The ultra engine does
            that by default (same approach as XRDAP-ESP, including the
            GPIO_ENABLE read-back and settle delay).

            The current GPIO fast path on this board keeps the SWDIO pad
            output enabled all the time (the A 2025 fork workaround, see
            DAP_config.h PIN_SWDIO_OUT_ENABLE/DISABLE on ESP32-S3), and that
            is the configuration known to pass OpenOCD bring-up on this board.
            Enable this option to make the ultra engine use the same
            "always driven" behaviour for the HIL A/B comparison if the
            protocol-correct turnaround fails on a particular target/wiring.

            Leave it disabled unless HIL debugging says otherwise.

endmenu
